WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.625
Vowels...

00:00:01.708 --> 00:00:05.250
We use them every day, but how much
do we really know about them?

00:00:07.292 --> 00:00:11.042
In the English language
we have five specific vowel letters,

00:00:11.292 --> 00:00:13.917
each with its own unique personality.

00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:18.250
First off on this linguistic A-list
is, well, it's A...

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:21.458
about as popular and versatile
a letter as you can get.

00:00:21.542 --> 00:00:24.958
A balances representing an impressive
seven different vowel sounds

00:00:25.042 --> 00:00:26.583
with the glitz and the glamour

00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:30.292
that comes from being the
English language's very first letter.

00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:35.000
A is literally the alpha
of our entire alphabet.

00:00:35.083 --> 00:00:37.458
It's the highest grade a teacher
can give you for your work,

00:00:37.542 --> 00:00:39.250
we have A-list celebrities,

00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:42.458
and many of us grew up watching
The A-Team save the day

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:43.875
on a regular basis.

00:00:44.125 --> 00:00:48.000
Some scientists believe that A has an
entrenched motivating impact on us,

00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:50.750
and can improve performance
at work or creatively.

00:00:51.542 --> 00:00:52.583
A is quite playful.

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:55.417
It features in a lot of words
that are just, well, fun to say,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.875
like llama, aardvark,

00:00:56.958 --> 00:00:58.417
handbags,
balaclava,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Madagascar, jazz hands,

00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:00.917
to name a few.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.333
And A isn't too interested
in numbers either.

00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:06.333
If you spelt out every number
from one upwards

00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:11.250
you wouldn't be using an A until
you got all the way up to 1,000.

00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:14.042
E, in contrast,
is much more grounded.

00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:16.417
It's the only vowel
in our most-used word - the.

00:01:16.958 --> 00:01:20.417
And it's also the most-used letter
in our entire alphabet,

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.667
appearing in 11%
of all the words we use.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.500
It's the second most likely key

00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:27.792
to break on your keyboard
from overuse.

00:01:28.208 --> 00:01:29.667
E is essential to storytelling -

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.792
it allows us
to write in the past tense.

00:01:34.208 --> 00:01:35.958
When E is left to its own devices,

00:01:36.042 --> 00:01:38.917
it takes a deep interest
in the natural world...

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:45.000
endlessly green elements,
stems, trees, weeds, levees etc.

00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:48.292
I is very concerned
with its appearance.

00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:51.833
It was the first letter to receive a
dot when in its lower case form

00:01:51.917 --> 00:01:55.542
and it's the only letter that is
always capitalised when on its own.

00:01:55.917 --> 00:01:58.667
It's also the first word in the
shortest complete sentence

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.625
in the English language - I am.

00:02:00.708 --> 00:02:03.333
It's great for active verbs,
and when on its own,

00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:07.208
it appears in a host of words
related to excess and desire.

00:02:07.583 --> 00:02:10.042
Without it, you couldn't have
kissing, or drinking,

00:02:10.125 --> 00:02:15.667
or hitting, or spilling,
or hardly any illicit thrills at all.

00:02:18.042 --> 00:02:19.875
Out of all the symbols
we use for vowels,

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.167
O is the one that appears
the most in other languages,

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.458
even ones that developed completely
independently of each other.

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:27.833
The shape of the letter O

00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:30.292
mirrors the shape of your mouth
as you make the sound,

00:02:30.375 --> 00:02:31.417
and it's thought that's why

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.708
it appears in so many
different languages.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.458
This constant circular shape
with no beginning or end

00:02:37.542 --> 00:02:40.083
offers some of our
most mysterious words...

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:41.875
things like the moon,

00:02:41.958 --> 00:02:43.250
spooky howls,

00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:44.833
grottos, horror,

00:02:44.917 --> 00:02:45.750
ghosts,

00:02:45.833 --> 00:02:47.250
groans, moans

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:48.625
and bones, too.

00:02:49.458 --> 00:02:51.375
U is much more scientific and,

00:02:51.458 --> 00:02:53.458
partly due to its
lateness in the alphabet,

00:02:53.542 --> 00:02:56.000
always has one eye on the future.

00:02:56.458 --> 00:02:58.708
It's an abbreviation for university,

00:02:58.792 --> 00:03:00.917
for uranium,
and is linked to submarines.

00:03:01.625 --> 00:03:03.625
It provides us with some
pleasant words,

00:03:03.708 --> 00:03:07.125
but also some of our
most unpleasant ones.

00:03:08.042 --> 00:03:10.375
U also works well with other letters.

00:03:10.458 --> 00:03:12.542
It can be completely silent
when it wants to be,

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:15.708
and it can be rare to get three
vowels together in a row,

00:03:15.792 --> 00:03:18.333
but when it happens,
in most instances,

00:03:18.417 --> 00:03:21.042
U is in there
holding it all together.

00:03:21.708 --> 00:03:24.250
So there you have it.
The English language's vowels -

00:03:24.333 --> 00:03:26.208
essential to everyday life,

00:03:26.417 --> 00:03:28.583
each one packed full of personality.

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:30.250
You'd best learn to live with them,

00:03:30.458 --> 00:03:33.500
because you can't speak without them.

